#
# The west manifest project filter: which Zephyr modules get cloned.
#
# ONE FILE, TWO CONSUMERS, because the alternative is two copies that drift:
#
#   provision/user/30-zephyr-workspace.sh   the board's ~/zephyrproject
#   .github/workflows/ci.yml                the CI workspace
#
# The Zephyr VERSION cannot be shared this way - west needs it inside west.yml
# for CI and on the `west init` command line for the board - so that one is
# written twice and gated by tools/check-zephyr-pin.sh. The filter has no such
# constraint, so it is shared outright and there is nothing to keep in sync.
#
# WHY CI FILTERS AT ALL, when a runner has the disk for everything.
# `west update` clones each project separately, and the cost is per-repo, not
# per-byte: 68 projects took 159s, almost all of it connection overhead on
# modules for hardware this board does not have. Filtering to what an STM32
# target actually builds from cuts that to roughly a third.
#
# It also closes a divergence. The board built ~20 modules while CI built 68,
# and this repository's own rule is that a CI job which compiles a different
# Zephyr from the board is worse than no CI job. That applied to the version;
# it applies to the module set for the same reason.
#
# HOW TO CHANGE IT: add the module, then build. `-hal_.*` drops every vendor
# HAL and the `+` entries add back the ones an STM32U585 needs; a new dependency
# usually shows up as a missing Kconfig symbol or a missing device-tree binding
# rather than as an obvious "module not found".
#
# Format: the first line below that is neither blank nor a comment is the
# filter, passed verbatim to `west config manifest.project-filter`.
-hal_.*,+hal_stm32,+hal_st,-lvgl,-cmsis-dsp,-cmsis-nn,-lora-basics-modem,-loramac-node,-acpica,-hostap,-openthread,-nrf_wifi,-trusted-firmware-m,-trusted-firmware-a,-tf-m-tests,-psa-arch-tests,-nrf_hw_models,-edtt,-net-tools
